<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hagar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Agar, Hajar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hagar is an Egyptian woman in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-genesis">Book of Genesis</a>, the concubine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a> and the mother of his firstborn son Ishmael. Genesis says that Sarah, believing herself barren, gave her to Abraham, that tension followed, and that Hagar fled into the desert, where an angel told her to return and promised her many descendants. After Isaac's birth she and Ishmael were sent away and, according to Genesis, God showed her a well. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> does not name her, but Islamic tradition describes Hajar as a maid who ran seven times between Safa and Marwah and found the Zamzam well, an act recalled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a> rites. Ishmael is generally taken as the ancestor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hagar.jpg" alt="Hagar" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: François-Joseph Navez · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:François-Joseph_Navez_-_Agar_et_Ismaël_dans_le_désert_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-genesis">Book of Genesis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a>, who regarded herself as barren, sought a way to fulfil God's promise that Abram would have descendants, and offered Hagar to him as a concubine. When Hagar knew she was pregnant she began to despise Sarai, and Sarai mistreated her, so that Hagar fled towards Shur. At a spring in the desert an angel of the LORD told her to return and submit, and promised that her offspring would be too many to count. She was told to name her son Ishmael, and afterwards called God El Roi, which is variously rendered as the god of sight or the god who saw her.</p><p>After Isaac was born, Sarah found the teenage Ishmael mocking her son at a feast held when Isaac was weaned, and demanded that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a> send Hagar and Ishmael away, declaring that Ishmael would not share Isaac's inheritance. Abraham was distressed, but God told him to heed his wife, since the promise would be carried out through Isaac while Ishmael would also become a great nation. Abraham gave Hagar bread and water and sent them into the wilderness of Beersheba. When the water was gone, an angel opened her eyes to a well, and she later found her son a wife from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in the Desert of Paran.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Interpreters have read Hagar in many ways. Rabbinical commentary held that she was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">Pharaoh</a>'s daughter, given to Sarah as a servant, and some commentators identified her with Keturah, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a> married after Sarah's death; others, among them the Rashbam and Nachmanides, held the two to be different women. In the Epistle to the Galatians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-the-apostle">Paul the Apostle</a> made her an allegory of bondage under the law, set against Sarah and the free heavenly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. Augustine of Hippo took her as a sign of the earthly city.</p><p>Islamic tradition names her Hajar and describes a maid or wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a> whom God commanded him to settle in the desert near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>. She ran seven times between the hills of Safa and Marwah in search of water until an angel caused the Zamzam Well to spring from the ground. Pilgrims repeat the seven runs during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a>, and some drink Zamzam water in her memory. In later centuries writers and artists, especially in the 19th century, often portrayed her sympathetically as an unjustly exiled woman, and since the 1970s some Israeli parents have given the name Hagar to daughters as a sign of reconciliation with the Arab world.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hagar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
